## Is carpal tunnel syndrome a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. Mild symptoms that settle with treatment are unlikely to be covered, but long-term numbness, pain or weakness that affects everyday tasks can be.

## Reasonable adjustments

- voice recognition software or a different keyboard and mouse
- more breaks, or changing tasks through the day
- tools with less vibration, or less time using them
- time off for surgery and a phased return

**Access to Work** can help pay for equipment.

## If work caused it

Carpal tunnel syndrome caused by using **hand-held vibrating tools** at work is a recognised work-related disease. If you were an employee, you may be able to claim **Industrial Injuries Disablement Benefit**. Your employer also has a legal duty to control **vibration** at work.

## Benefits

Most people with carpal tunnel syndrome will not qualify for PIP. It may help if severe symptoms in both hands affect activities such as **preparing food**, **dressing** (buttons and zips) or **eating** (cutting food), on more than half of days.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
